date: '2026-11-10'
lang: en
bibliography: bibliography.bib
abstract: 'A historical astronomical model is a geometrical construction with a kinematic variable. Texts about
  such

  models do four things at once: they describe a geometry, give it numbers, show it in one position, and reason

  about it. We present astra/1, a small language that writes each of the four as a text of its own kind. A

  *model* is the pure geometry: parameter symbols, a kinematic variable, a construction without coordinates,

  observables; it holds no values. A *parametrisation* gives the parameters values, each with the page it comes

  from; one model has many. A *state* is a value of the kinematic variable; model, parametrisation and state

  together give a configuration. A *superposition* places several models in one plane and identifies their

  inputs. A *derivation* is the operation that follows construction: hypotheses, auxiliary constructions,

  propositions derived from named premises by named rules, quantities computed or determined, a conclusion. Four

  questions are asked of a derivation: whether it is grounded, whether every proposition is true, whether every

  step is an instance of the rule it names with its configuration conditions holding, and whether the conclusion

  rests on what is cited. Every text names three roles that must not be confused: whose model it is, which text''s

  use of it is analysed, and who wrote the description. We describe Ptolemy''s solar model, in its eccentric and

  its epicyclic form, as Noel Swerdlow uses it in *The Renaissance of Astronomy* (2026). The diagram that shows

  both forms is a superposition, and their equivalence is the conclusion of a derivation under hypotheses on the

  radii: equivalence in position when the radii are equal, by Elements I.28 and I.33, and in direction when they

  are proportional, by VI.6 and I.29. The parallelogram argument does not apply at the apsides, where the

  parallelogram collapses; the derivation therefore argues by three cases. Ptolemy''s reported eccentricity

  and apogee follow from his rounded arcs of mean motion, not from the day counts of the seasons.

  '
keywords:
- history of astronomy
- Ptolemy's solar model
- geometrical reasoning
- domain-specific language
- model equivalence
- scholarly reference objects
license: CC BY-NC-ND 4.0
