## A diagram and what it does not say {#sec-intro}

Chapter 1 of Noel Swerdlow's *The Renaissance of Astronomy* [@swerdlow2026] sets out Ptolemy's solar theory
with three diagrams. The first (Fig. 1.2, printed page 20) shows two models of the sun's inequality at once.
In the *eccentric* form the sun S moves uniformly on a circle whose centre E is displaced from the earth O. In
the *epicyclic* form a small circle, the epicycle, carries the sun; its centre C moves uniformly on a circle
about O, while the epicycle turns so that its radius keeps the direction of the line through the apogee. The
prose on the facing page says how the two are related: "To construct the epicyclic model, complete
parallelogram OESC and let C be the center of an epicycle of radius r = e" on a circle "of radius R′ = R"
(p. 19). The models are Ptolemy's; Swerdlow says so in the same paragraph ("Ptolemy sets out eccentric and
epicyclic models for this inequality"). What we analyse is Swerdlow's use of them.

A reader who wants to understand the diagram has to take apart four things that the page presents together.

*A geometry.* Each form is a construction: points, circles, an angle that varies. Nothing in it depends on how
large the eccentricity is.

*Numbers.* The values R = 60, e = 2;29,30 ≈ 2;30 and λ~A~ = 65;30° stand two pages later (p. 22), and they are
Ptolemy's. The same paragraph gives other values for the same geometry, al-Battānī's. The printed diagrams use
a third set: an eccentricity many times too large, so that the drawing can be read.

*A position.* The diagram shows the construction for one value of the mean anomaly, about 41°. The same
geometry with the same numbers appears again in Fig. 1.4 in another position and another orientation.

*An argument.* The diagram shows *two* models in one plane, and the claim about them is that they are
equivalent. The parallelogram is not a part of either model. It is the place of an argument, and the argument
has hypotheses, r = e and R′ = R, that the diagram cannot show.

An earlier version of this paper (1.1) did not keep these apart. It wrote geometry, Ptolemy's values and the
depicted position into one text; it described Fig. 1.2 as a "correspondence" from one model to the other with
a "parameter map"; it checked the steps of the argument only numerically; and it named Swerdlow, whose
exposition it followed, as the author of the models. This version corrects all four. The design now follows
five requirements.

1. **A model has no values.** The geometry is one text, checked over its whole parameter space. Values are
   another text, with an owner and sources. A position is an argument.
2. **Three roles are named and never mixed**: whose model it is, the text whose use of it is analysed, and who
   wrote the description.
3. **A diagram of two models is a superposition**, and what holds across the two is derived under stated
   hypotheses, not drawn in.
4. **Geometrical reasoning is an operation of its own**, after the construction: a derivation whose steps name
   their premises and their rules, and each step is checked for truth and for whether the rule licenses it.
5. **Every part is citable**, down to a step of a derivation.

@sec-kinds introduces the vocabulary with the eccentric form as the running example. @sec-language gives the
language, @sec-semantics its meaning, @sec-checks what can be said of each kind of text. @sec-super treats
superposition and equivalence, @sec-deriv derivation. @sec-derived describes what follows from the texts,
@sec-case the case study and what it found, @sec-repr the relation of model, representation and description.
@sec-related compares astra/1 with the work it learned from; @sec-limits states what is not done.

## Model, parametrisation, state {#sec-kinds}

Three questions can be asked of a historical astronomical model, and their answers are of different kinds.

*What is its geometry?* The eccentric form is: a circle whose centre E lies at some distance from the observer
O in some direction; a point S on the circle at an angle κ̄ from the apogee; the direction from O to S as what
is observed. Relations that follow from the construction alone — the line through apogee and perigee passes
through E and O; the mean longitude is the longitude of the apogee plus the mean anomaly — are true for every
eccentricity, every radius, every direction of the apogee, in every position. We call this the **model**. It
has parameter *symbols* and a **kinematic variable**, κ̄, and no values.

*What are its values?* Ptolemy found R = 60, e = 2;30 and λ~A~ = 65;30°. With them the model has numerical
consequences: the largest equation of centre is 2;23°. Al-Battānī found e = 2;4,45 and λ~A~ = 82;17°; the
largest equation is then 1;59°. A set of values for a model's parameters, each with the place where it stands
and with the name of whose values they are, is a **parametrisation**. One model has many.

*Where is everything?* That needs, besides values, a value of the kinematic variable: a **state**. A model
under a parametrisation in a state is a **configuration**: every point has a place. A diagram shows one
configuration; an animation runs through the states.

The three fix different things, stand in different places and support different statements (@tbl-kinds).

| | what it fixes | where it stands | what can be said of it |
|---|---|---|---|
| **model** | parameter symbols with units and admissible ranges; the kinematic variable; the construction; the observables | a `model` text | what holds for every admissible parameter value and every state |
| **parametrisation** | the values of the parameters, each with source or stated gap; whose values they are | a `parametrisation` text | what follows for these values |
| **state** | the value of the kinematic variable, or a time | an argument, `at kappa_bar = 41°` | where everything is: a configuration |

: Model, parametrisation and state. {#tbl-kinds}

Two more kinds of text do something with models. A **superposition** places several models in one plane
(@sec-super). A **derivation** reasons in a model or a superposition (@sec-deriv). A fifth kind, the
**figure**, says what is drawn: which context, which values, which state, which labels. @fig-kinds shows how
the kinds refer to one another.

![The kinds of text of astra/1 and what they refer to.](figures/pipeline.svg){#fig-kinds width=100%}

Every text carries three names. `attributed` says whose model, or whose values, it is. `witness` gives the
address of the text whose use of the model is analysed. `author` says who wrote the astra text. For the texts
of this paper these are Ptolemy, Swerdlow's pages, and the author of this paper. An exposition is not an
authorship: the language refuses an `author` that is a source address, and a model without `attributed` is not
documented.

## The language {#sec-language}

### Texts

An astra/1 text is a sequence of statements, one per line; the first names the kind of text. A `#` begins a
comment. Numbers are written as the sources write them: decimal (`12.5`) or sexagesimal, with a semicolon
after the integer part and commas between the fractional places, so that `2;30` is 2 + 30/60 and `365;14,48` is
365 + 14/60 + 48/3600. A degree sign makes an angle (`65;30°`). The written form of every number is kept.
Identifiers are ASCII letters, digits, underscores and the prime (`S'`, `R'`); the printed symbol is declared
separately (`display kappa_bar "κ̄"`). Addresses of sources are written `fr:…` (@sec-references).

@lst-eccentric is the complete model text of the eccentric form, comments removed. It contains no number that
is a value.

::: {#lst-eccentric}

```
model solar-eccentric "Ptolemy's solar model, eccentric form"
attributed "Ptolemy"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0049/p04 "the construction, p. 19"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0050/p03 "longitudes, anomalies and the
      equation, p. 20"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0051/fig02 "Fig. 1.4"
author "Gerd Graßhoff"
tradition ptolemaic
frame geocentric
param R  : length "radius of the eccentric"  in (1, 1000)
param e  : length "eccentricity, the distance OE"  in (0, R / 2)
param lamA  : angle  "longitude of the apogee"
param T  : time  "tropical year"  in (300, 400)
param kappa_0 : angle  "mean anomaly at t = 0"
display lamA "λ_A"
display kappa_0 "κ̄_0"
variable kappa_bar : angle "mean anomaly about E, counted from the apogee"
law kappa_bar = uniform(period: T, epoch: kappa_0) "the sun moves uniformly
      about E"
display kappa_bar "κ̄"
O  = observer  "earth"
E  = O + polar(e, lamA)  "centre of the eccentric"
ecc = circle(E, R)  "the eccentric"
A  = E + polar(R, lamA)  "apogee"
B  = E - polar(R, lamA)  "perigee"
S  = E + polar(R, lamA + kappa_bar)  "sun"
body sun at S
observe lam_bar = direction(E, S)  "mean longitude"
observe lam  = direction(O, S)  "true longitude"
observe kappa  = lam - lamA  "true anomaly about O"
observe c  = lam_bar - lam  "equation of centre, mean minus true longitude"
display lam_bar "λ̄_S"
display lam "λ_S"
display kappa "κ"
proposition p1: equal(lam_bar, lamA + kappa_bar) "the mean longitude is the
      longitude of the apogee plus the mean anomaly"
proposition p2: equal(kappa, kappa_bar - c)  "the true anomaly is the mean
      anomaly less the equation"
proposition p5: equal(lam, lam_bar - c)  "the true longitude is the mean
      longitude less the equation"
proposition p3: collinear(A, E, B)  "the apsidal line passes through E"
proposition p4: collinear(A, O, B)  "and through O"
```

The model `solar-eccentric`: Ptolemy's solar model in its eccentric form. Parameters are symbols with units and
ranges; `variable` declares the kinematic variable, `law` its link to time; `proposition` states what is then
checked over the parameter space.
:::

The values are other texts (@lst-par). The second is partial: the paragraph that reports al-Battānī's values
gives no year and no epoch, and the text does not invent them.

::: {#lst-par}

```
parametrisation solar-eccentric.ptolemy of solar-eccentric "Ptolemy's values"
attributed "Ptolemy"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05
author "Gerd Graßhoff"
R  = 60  "the norm of the radius"  source fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05
e  = 2;30  "2;29,30 rounded to 2;30"  source fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05
lamA  = 65;30°  source fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05
T  = 365;14,48  source fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0047/p03
kappa_0 = 0°  gap "the pages give no epoch; t is counted from a passage through
      the apogee"

parametrisation solar-eccentric.battani of solar-eccentric "al-Battānī's values"
attributed "al-Battānī"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p06
author "Gerd Graßhoff"
R  = 60  gap "the norm R = 60 is not repeated in the paragraph; e is given in
      its units"
e  = 2;4,45  source fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p06
lamA = 82;17°  source fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p06
```

Two parametrisations of the one model. `0052/p05` is block 5 of scan page 52, printed page 22; the reference register counts the running head as the first block. A value
without a source needs a `gap` that says why.
:::

### Statements

@tbl-statements lists the statements by kind of text. Each has a fixed form. A statement in a text of the
wrong kind is refused with a message that says where it belongs (@lst-refused).

| kind | statements |
|---|---|
| all | `attributed`, `witness`, `author`, `display` |
| model | `param … : <unit> [in (lo, hi)]`, `variable`, `law`, construction `<name> = <expr>`, `observe`, `body`, `proposition`, `tradition`, `frame` |
| parametrisation | value `<param> = <expr>` with `source fr:…` or `gap "why"`, `use` |
| superposition | `place <model> as <Prefix>`, `identify <P.x> = <Q.y>` |
| figure | `with <parametrisation>`, `drawn with`, `at <variable> = <angle>`, `up`, `fit`, `speed`, `label`, `hide` |
| derivation | `given`, `construct`, `derive … from … by …`, `compute`, `determine`, `conclude` |

: The statements of astra/1 by kind of text; the full forms are in the specification. {#tbl-statements tbl-colwidths="[22,78]"}

::: {#lst-refused}

```
param R = 60 length "radius"
  → a model holds no values: declare `param R : <unit>` here and give the value
        in a parametrisation
R = 60 source fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05
  → a model holds no values and no sources; values and their sources stand in a
        parametrisation
at kappa_bar = 41°
  → `at` belongs in a figure text, not in a model
author fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026
  → `author` names who wrote this text; the text analysed is the `witness`
identify Ecc.S = Epi.S'
  → only parameters of one unit, kinematic variables and the observer can be
        identified; that two constructed points coincide is a proposition to
        derive
```

What the parser and the superposition refuse, with their messages.
:::

Expressions use the four arithmetic operators, parentheses, names and functions (@tbl-functions). The
predicates of propositions are `parallel`, `perpendicular`, `coincident`, `collinear`, `on` and `equal`.

| function | gives | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `observer` | point | the origin of the model frame |
| `polar(r, θ)` | displacement | length r in direction θ; `P + polar(r, θ)` is a point |
| `circle(P, r)`, `line(P, Q)`, `ray(P, θ)` | circle, line | lines are directed |
| `intersect(k, l, ±)` | point | circle with line or circle; the sign chooses the branch |
| `foot(P, l)`, `midpoint(P, Q)`, `rotate(P, about: A, by: θ)`, `reflect(P, l)` | point | |
| `ellipse(F₁, F₂, a)`, `on_ellipse(k, E)`, `tangent(P, k, ±)`, `seen_from(Q, k, θ)` | ellipse, point | Kepler's orbit; the equant |
| `direction(P, Q)`, `distance(P, Q)` | angle, length | undefined when P and Q coincide |
| `angle(P, V, Q)` | angle | the unsigned angle at V, between 0° and 180°: the angle of Euclid's propositions |
| `true_anomaly(M, e)` | angle | from Kepler's equation |
| `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `asin`, `atan2`, `sqrt`, `abs`, `norm360`, `norm180` | number, angle | |

: The functions of astra/1. {#tbl-functions}

An intersection of a circle and a line has two points, and a model must say which one it means: for a circle
and a directed line, `+` is the point farther along the line's direction; for two circles, the point to the
left of the line from the first centre to the second. A missing sign is an error, not a guess.

### Types and the abstract syntax

Every name and every expression has a type: a number, an angle, a length, a time, a rate, a point, a
displacement, a line, a circle or an ellipse. An angle cannot be added to a length; a circle needs a point and
a length; a product of two lengths is allowed on the way to a length or in a comparison of squares. Names are
defined before they are used, so a construction is a directed graph without cycles by the way it is written, as
in GCLC [@janicic2010gclc]. Nothing is placed by coordinates except the observer, the origin.

What the tools work on is the *abstract syntax*: the tree of statements and expressions that the parser
builds, independent of spacing and comments. A superposition merges the trees of its members; a derivation
extends the tree of its context; a rule of derivation is a pair of trees with variables (@sec-deriv).

## What a model means {#sec-semantics}

A context — a model, a superposition, or a derivation with its auxiliary constructions — compiles to a list of
*operations* in the order in which they must be computed. Each operation has a name, the names of the
operations it reads, and a type. The operation set has forty-two operations (@tbl-ops) and is the only place
where the geometry is defined. A value is a number or a short list of numbers: a point is its two coordinates,
a circle its centre and radius, a line a point and a unit direction. Coordinates appear here, inside the
meaning of a model, never in its text.

| operations | what they compute |
|---|---|
| `param`, `var` | the inputs: a parameter, read from the parametrisation; a kinematic variable, read from the state |
| `const`, `id` | a literal; a named value |
| `add`, `sub`, `mul`, `div`, `neg`, `abs`, `norm360`, `norm180` | arithmetic on numbers and displacements |
| `observer`, `polar`, `circle`, `line`, `ray` | elementary placements |
| `x_cl`, `x_cc`, `x_el`, `tangent`, `seen_from` | intersections and contacts; no solution gives "not a number" |
| `foot`, `midpoint`, `rotate`, `reflect`, `ellipse`, `on_ellipse` | further constructions |
| `direction`, `distance` | what an observer measures; undefined for coincident points |
| `kepler_E`, `true_anom` | Kepler's equation, solved by Newton's method |
| `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `atan2`, `asin`, `sqrt` | functions for formulae |
| `res_parallel`, `res_perp`, `res_collinear`, `res_on` | residuals of predicates |

: The forty-two operations (`spec/ops.yaml`), grouped. {#tbl-ops}

There is one evaluation. Given the operation list of a context, parameter values p and a state s, it returns
the value of every object:

  configuration(operations, p, s).

The model *is* this function with p and s left open. A parametrisation fixes p. A state fixes s, either by
naming the kinematic variable (κ̄ = 41°) or by a time, which the variable's law turns into a value: for the
uniform law, κ̄ = κ̄₀ + 360° · t / T, with the period T and the epoch κ̄₀ as ordinary parameters. Nothing has a
default. A parameter without a value is an error as soon as anything other than a range or a law needs it; a
state that fixes no variable is an error. So a partial parametrisation, like al-Battānī's, can still be used
for what it determines — the largest equation needs only R and e — and says what it cannot give.

Because a model has no values, statements about it are tested where it lives. The parameter space is sampled
at 48 points, each parameter in its declared range; a range that depends on another parameter, like that of
the eccentricity, `in (0, R / 2)`, is evaluated inside each sample. The states are the kinematic variable in
72 steps of 5°. A proposition is turned into a *residual*, a number that is zero when it holds: the distance
of two points (`coincident`), the sine of the angle between two lines (`parallel`), the cosine
(`perpendicular`), the sine of the bend at the middle of three points (`collinear`), the absolute difference of
two scalars, angles reduced to the range from −180° to 180° (`equal`). A proposition holds when its residual
stays within 10⁻⁹ in all 3,456 configurations, lengths taken relative to the largest length parameter. This is
evidence, not proof.

## What can be said of a text {#sec-checks}

Each kind of text has its own check, in levels that presuppose one another (@tbl-levels). The report gives the
level reached and, for the first level not reached, every reason.

| kind | levels |
|---|---|
| model | well-formed · constructible · astronomical · kinematic · consistent · documented |
| parametrisation | well-formed · complete · admissible · attested |
| superposition | well-formed · constructible · superposed · consistent |
| figure | well-formed · drawable · labelled |
| derivation | well-formed · grounded · true · justified · conclusive |

: The checks by kind of text. {#tbl-levels}

A model is *constructible* when every object has a solution in every sampled configuration; *astronomical*
when it has one observer, a body, an observable direction from the observer to the body and a declared frame;
*kinematic* when a kinematic variable reaches a body through the construction; *consistent* when its stated
propositions hold in every sampled configuration; *documented* when it names whose model it is, a witness and
an author, and gives every symbol and object a meaning. A parametrisation is *complete* when every parameter
has a value, *admissible* when every value lies in its range, *attested* when every value has a source or a
stated gap. @lst-check shows two reports.

::: {#lst-check}

```
$ famulus-astra check solar-eccentric
solar-eccentric: model, level documented (6/6)
  ✓ well-formed
  ✓ constructible
  ✓ astronomical
  ✓ kinematic
  ✓ consistent
  ✓ documented
  ✓ p1: equal(lam_bar, lamA + kappa_bar)  max residual 5.7e-14 over 3456
        configurations
  ✓ p2: equal(kappa, kappa_bar - c)  max residual 1.1e-13 over 3456
        configurations
  ✓ p5: equal(lam, lam_bar - c)  max residual 5.7e-14 over 3456 configurations
  ✓ p3: collinear(A, E, B)  max residual 1.5e-16 over 3456 configurations
  ✓ p4: collinear(A, O, B)  max residual 1.3e-16 over 3456 configurations

$ famulus-astra check solar-eccentric.battani
solar-eccentric.battani: parametrisation, level well-formed (1/4)
  ✓ well-formed
  ✗ complete
      T (tropical year) has no value
      kappa_0 (mean anomaly at t = 0) has no value
  · admissible (not reached)
  · attested (not reached)
  gap: R = 60: the norm R = 60 is not repeated in the paragraph; e is given in
        its units
```

The reports for the eccentric model and for al-Battānī's parametrisation. The second is incomplete, and says
which values the page does not give.
:::

A proposition that is true for Ptolemy's values and not in general, such as that the eccentricity is a
twenty-fourth of the radius, is not a property of the model. Stated in the model it fails the level
*consistent*, with the sampled values that break it. It belongs to a parametrisation, as a computed
consequence (@sec-deriv).

## Superposition and equivalence {#sec-super}

Fig. 1.2 shows the two forms of Ptolemy's solar model in one plane. That is a superposition (@lst-super).

::: {#lst-super}

```
superposition solar-eccentric-epicyclic "Eccentric and epicyclic form of
      Ptolemy's solar model, superposed"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0050/fig01 "Fig. 1.2"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0051/fig01 "Fig. 1.3"
author "Gerd Graßhoff"
place solar-eccentric as Ecc
place solar-epicyclic as Epi
identify Ecc.O = Epi.O  "one earth"
identify Ecc.lamA = Epi.lamA  "one apsidal direction"
identify Ecc.T = Epi.T  "one year"
identify Ecc.kappa_0 = Epi.kappa_0  "one epoch"
identify Ecc.kappa_bar = Epi.kappa_bar "one mean anomaly"
display Epi.lam "λ'_S"
display Ecc.lam "λ_S"
```

The superposition `solar-eccentric-epicyclic`. It is the scene of Figs. 1.2 and 1.3.
:::

Every name of a member gets the member's prefix (`Ecc.S`, `Epi.S'`). The names the text identifies become one:
the observer, the longitude of the apogee, the year, the epoch, the mean anomaly. Identification says that the
two models are about the same earth, the same apsidal direction and the same mean motion. It adds no geometry.
Only inputs — parameters of one unit, kinematic variables — and the observer can be identified. A text that
identifies the two suns is refused (@lst-refused): that two constructed points coincide is a proposition, and
propositions are derived.

A superposition is again a model: pure geometry over the parameters of both members, checked like any model.
In it the two suns are two points. For some parameter values they coincide, for others they lie on one ray
from the earth, for most they do neither. Nothing in the superposition says which. @fig-two shows the two
members separately, @fig-super the superposition under the values of Fig. 1.2 and of Fig. 1.3.

![The two members drawn separately: the eccentric form (left) and the epicyclic form (right). The pages give
the epicyclic form no values of its own; it is drawn with drawing values, and the corner says
so.](figures/two-models.png){#fig-two width=100%}

**Equivalence** is a relation between two models. It holds relative to hypotheses and relative to what is
compared. The pages contain two equivalences of the same two models:

| | hypotheses | the models are equivalent in |
|---|---|---|
| Fig. 1.2 | R′ = R and r = e | *position*: the two suns are one point in every state |
| Fig. 1.3 | R′/r = R/e | *direction*: the sun is seen from the earth in the same direction; the distances differ |

: The two equivalences of the eccentric and the epicyclic form. {#tbl-equiv}

Each is the conclusion of a derivation in the superposition. Equivalence is distinct from **identity**. Two
texts are the same model when they are the same geometry up to renaming, which a structural comparison tests
(@sec-compare). The eccentric and the epicyclic form are different models. Under hypotheses they agree in what
is compared, and they stay different in the rest: in the one the sun moves uniformly on a circle about E, in
the other its motion is compounded of two. Swerdlow notes that only Kepler drew a consequence from this
difference (p. 20).

![The superposition under two parametrisations. Left, the values of Fig. 1.2 (R′ = R, r = e): the two suns are
one point. Right, the values of Fig. 1.3 (R′/r = R/e): two suns on one ray from the earth. Both are drawn with
an exaggerated eccentricity, as the printed figures are.](figures/super.png){#fig-super width=100%}

The superposition in motion under the values of Fig. 1.2: the mean anomaly runs through a revolution, and the
slider below the figure fades from one member to the other.

{{< famulus fr:astra/solar-fig-1-2 animation >}}

## Derivation {#sec-deriv}

After the construction of a model comes the operation of derivation: geometrical reasoning about what has been
constructed. A derivation works in a context, a model or a superposition, and consists of operations
(@tbl-derops).

| operation | what it does |
|---|---|
| `given h: equal(<param>, <expr>)` | a hypothesis; it restricts the parameter space |
| `construct F = <expr>` | an auxiliary construction, added to the context |
| `derive d: <proposition> from <premises> by <rule>` | a proposition derived from named premises by a named rule |
| `show A: <proposition> … qed from <steps>` | a sub-argument: the claim first, then the steps that establish it; its inside cannot be cited from outside |
| `cases` / `case n: when <condition>` / `qed by cases` | a claim argued separately under conditions that together must cover every configuration |
| `suppose n: <condition>` | restricts the steps that follow to the configurations in which the condition holds |
| `conclude <proposition> from <premises>` | the proposition the derivation is for; `equivalent(P, Q, position)` or `equivalent(P, Q, direction)` for an equivalence of two members |
| `compute x = <expr>` | a quantity under a parametrisation, compared with a reported value |
| `determine p, q from …` | parameters determined from observed intervals through the model |

: The operations of a derivation. {#tbl-derops tbl-colwidths="[45,55]"}

A premise is a given or an earlier step, by name; the construction of an object (`def Ecc.S`); or a
proposition stated in the model (`prop p1`). A rule is a proposition of Euclid (`Elements I.33`), a lemma, a
definition of trigonometry, `common notion 1`, `construction` or `algebra`. The propositions of a step are
those of a model, with two more: `less(a, b)`, and `approximately(a, b, small: <ratio>, order: n)` for a step
that holds to a stated order in a small ratio (rule `expansion`). The notions are set out together in
[@grasshoff2026fundamentals]; sub-arguments, cases and suppositions were added to the language for the
reconstructions in [@grasshoff2026twocases] and [@grasshoff2026kepler].

### The derivation that Fig. 1.2 exhibits

The page names the parallelogram OESC. It does not give the steps. @lst-deriv gives them, with the rule that
licenses each. The claim that the two suns are one point is argued in three cases: off the apsidal line, at
the apogee, at the perigee.

::: {#lst-deriv}

```
derivation solar-equivalence-equal-radii in solar-eccentric-epicyclic "The two
      forms place the sun at the same point when R' = R and r = e"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0049/p04 "the parallelogram OESC, p. 19"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0050/fig01 "Fig. 1.2"
author "Gerd Graßhoff"
given h1: equal(Epi.R', Ecc.R) "the deferent has the radius of the eccentric"
given h2: equal(Epi.r, Ecc.e)  "the epicycle has the eccentricity as its radius"
show same: coincident(Ecc.S, Epi.S') "the sun of the eccentric form and the sun
      of the epicyclic form are one point"
  derive d3: equal(distance(Ecc.E, Ecc.S), Ecc.R)  from def Ecc.S by
        construction  "S lies on the eccentric"
  derive d4: equal(distance(O, Epi.C), Epi.R')  from def Epi.C by construction
        "C lies on the deferent"
  derive d8: equal(distance(O, Ecc.E), Ecc.e)  from def Ecc.E by construction
        "OE is the eccentricity"
  derive d9: equal(distance(Epi.C, Epi.S'), Epi.r)  from def Epi.S' by
        construction "S' lies on the epicycle"
  cases "by the position of the sun: off the apsidal line, at the apogee, at the
        perigee"
  case generic: when noncollinear(O, Ecc.E, Ecc.S) "off the apsidal line, OESC
        is a parallelogram"
    show sides: parallel(line(Ecc.E, Ecc.S), line(O, Epi.C)) "ES and OC are
          parallel"
      derive d1: equal(angle(Ecc.A, Ecc.E, Ecc.S), angle(Epi.A', O, Epi.C)) from
            def Ecc.A, def Ecc.S, def Epi.A', def Epi.C by construction "both
            angles are the mean anomaly κ̄, counted from the apsidal line"
      derive d2: parallel(line(Ecc.E, Ecc.S), line(O, Epi.C)) from d1 by
            Elements I.28 "the apsidal line falls on ES and OC and makes equal
            angles with them on the same side"
    qed from d2
    derive d5: equal(distance(Ecc.E, Ecc.S), distance(O, Epi.C)) from d3, d4, h1
          by common notion 1 "ES = R = R' = OC"
    show joins: parallel(line(O, Ecc.E), line(Epi.C, Ecc.S)) "OE and CS are
          parallel: OESC is a parallelogram"
      derive d6: parallel(line(O, Ecc.E), line(Epi.C, Ecc.S)) from sides, d5 by
            Elements I.33 "the lines joining the ends of the equal and parallel
            ES and OC"
    qed from d6
    derive d7: equal(distance(O, Ecc.E), distance(Epi.C, Ecc.S)) from sides, d5
          by Elements I.33 "and they are equal"
    derive d10: equal(distance(Epi.C, Ecc.S), distance(Epi.C, Epi.S')) from d7,
          d8, h2, d9 by common notion 1 "CS = OE = e = r = CS'"
    derive d11: parallel(line(Epi.C, Epi.S'), line(O, Ecc.E)) from def Epi.S',
          def Ecc.E by construction "the radius of the epicycle keeps the
          direction of the apsidal line"
    derive d12: coincident(Ecc.S, Epi.S') from joins, d11, d10 by lemma same ray
          "S and S' lie from C along the parallel to OE, the same way, at the
          same distance"
  qed from d12
  case apogee: when collinear(O, Ecc.E, Ecc.S), between(Ecc.E, O, Ecc.S) "at the
        apogee the four points lie on the apsidal line, E between O and S"
    derive a1: equal(distance(O, Ecc.S), distance(O, Ecc.E) + distance(Ecc.E,
          Ecc.S)) by lemma segment addition "OS = OE + ES"
    derive a2: equal(distance(O, Epi.S'), distance(O, Epi.C) + distance(Epi.C,
          Epi.S')) by lemma segment addition "OS' = OC + CS'"
    derive a3: equal(distance(O, Ecc.S), distance(O, Epi.S')) from a1, a2, d3,
          d4, d8, d9, h1, h2 by algebra "e + R = R' + r"
    derive a4: parallel(line(O, Ecc.S), line(O, Ecc.E)) from apogee by lemma one
          line "S lies on the apsidal line"
    derive a5: collinear(O, Ecc.E, Epi.S') from def Epi.C, def Epi.S', def Ecc.E
          by construction "and so does S'"
    derive a6: parallel(line(O, Epi.S'), line(O, Ecc.E)) from a5 by lemma one
          line
    derive a7: coincident(Ecc.S, Epi.S') from a4, a6, a3 by lemma same ray "S
          and S' lie from O along the apsidal line, the same way, at the same
          distance"
  qed from a7
  case perigee: when collinear(O, Ecc.E, Ecc.S), between(O, Ecc.E, Ecc.S) "at
        the perigee the four points lie on the apsidal line, O between E and S"
    derive p1: equal(distance(Ecc.E, Ecc.S), distance(Ecc.E, O) + distance(O,
          Ecc.S)) by lemma segment addition "ES = EO + OS"
    derive p2: equal(distance(Epi.C, O), distance(Epi.C, Epi.S') +
          distance(Epi.S', O)) by lemma segment addition "CO = CS' + S'O"
    derive p3: equal(distance(O, Ecc.S), distance(O, Epi.S')) from p1, p2, d3,
          d4, d8, d9, h1, h2 by algebra "R − e = R' − r"
    derive p4: parallel(line(O, Ecc.S), line(O, Ecc.E)) from perigee by lemma
          one line "S lies on the apsidal line"
    derive p5: collinear(O, Ecc.E, Epi.S') from def Epi.C, def Epi.S', def Ecc.E
          by construction "and so does S'"
    derive p6: parallel(line(O, Epi.S'), line(O, Ecc.E)) from p5 by lemma one
          line
    derive p7: coincident(Ecc.S, Epi.S') from p4, p6, p3 by lemma same ray "S
          and S' lie from O along the apsidal line, the same way, at the same
          distance"
  qed from p7
qed by cases
conclude equivalent(Ecc, Epi, position) from same "in every state the two forms
      have the sun at one point"
```

The derivation `solar-equivalence-equal-radii`, comments removed. Ecc and Epi are the two members of the
superposition.
:::

In words: the angles AES and A′OC are both the mean anomaly, so ES and OC are parallel (Elements I.28: "if a
straight line falling on two straight lines make the exterior angle equal to the interior and opposite angle on
the same side … the straight lines will be parallel"). They are equal, since R = R′. So the lines that join
their ends, OE and CS, are equal and parallel (I.33: "the straight lines joining equal and parallel straight
lines (at the extremities which are) in the same directions (respectively) are themselves also equal and
parallel") [@heath1908]. The sun S′ of the epicyclic form lies from C in the direction of the apsidal line at
the distance r, and r = e. So S and S′ lie from C along the same parallel, the same way, at the same distance:
they are one point. That is the first case. At the apogee and at the perigee the four points lie on the
apsidal line and there is no parallelogram; there OS = e + R and OS′ = R′ + r (at the perigee R − e and
R′ − r), which are equal under the two hypotheses, and both suns lie on the apsidal line on the same side of O.
@fig-steps shows three steps of the first case in the figure.

![Three steps in the scene of the derivation, each with the points and segments it speaks of in gold: d2, ES is
parallel to OC; d6, OE is parallel to CS; d12, S and S′ are one point.](figures/steps.png){#fig-steps width=100%}

### Four questions {#sec-four}

Four questions are asked of a derivation, each presupposing the one before.

**Is it grounded?** Every premise resolves to a given, an earlier step, a construction or a stated proposition.

**Is it true?** Every proposition holds in all sampled configurations that satisfy the givens. A given of the
form `equal(<parameter>, <expression>)` fixes that parameter by the others, so the samples lie on the part of
the parameter space where the hypotheses hold.

**Is it justified?** Every step must be licensed by the rule it names. There are four grades.

- *An instance of a rule.* A rule is a schema (@tbl-rules): variables for points, hypotheses, conclusions. A
  step is an instance when the variables can be bound to points of the context so that a conclusion of the rule
  is the step's proposition and each hypothesis of the rule is one of the cited premises. The match respects
  the symmetries of the predicates: `parallel` and `equal` are symmetric, a line and a distance do not depend
  on the order of their points, an angle is unsigned. For step d6 the binding is A = O, B = C, C = E, D = S,
  and the two hypotheses of I.33 are met by d2 and d5.
- *Common Notion 1.* The two sides of an equality are joined by the cited equalities.
- *Read off the construction.* The proposition speaks only of the cited objects and what they are built from,
  and it holds over the whole parameter space **without** the givens. That ES = R is read off the construction
  of S. A proposition that needs a hypothesis cannot pass as read off.
- *Calculation.* An equality from cited equalities. It is confirmed numerically; the calculation is not
  replayed.

A sub-argument is justified when the steps it closes with establish its claim, and an argument by cases when
every case reaches the claim and the conditions of the cases cover every sampled configuration. The steps of a
case are checked only in the configurations that satisfy its condition. A step that is true and none of these
is reported as *true, not justified*: a gap in the reasoning.

**Is it conclusive?** For every given, the parameter space is sampled once more with that given lifted. A
proposition that then fails *needs* the given. If the given is not cited on the way to that proposition, it is
a **tacit premise**, and the derivation is not conclusive. Givens that the conclusion does not need, and steps
that are not on the way to it, are noted.

@lst-dcheck is the report for the derivation of Fig. 1.2, and @lst-fail what the checks say for six altered
versions of it.

::: {#lst-dcheck}

