The same derivation with one line altered in each case, and what the checks report.
:::

| rule | kind | case stated | Heath 1908 |
|---|---|---|---|
| Elements I.13 | proposition | a straight line set up on a straight line makes angles equal to two right angles | vol. I, p. 275 |
| Elements I.28 | proposition | exterior angle equal to the interior and opposite angle: the lines are parallel | vol. I, p. 309 |
| Elements I.29 | proposition | parallels make equal alternate angles | vol. I, p. 311 |
| Elements I.30 | proposition | lines parallel to one line are parallel | vol. I, p. 314 |
| Elements I.32 | proposition | the exterior angle of a triangle | vol. I, p. 316 |
| Elements I.33 | proposition | the lines joining equal and parallel lines | vol. I, p. 322 |
| Elements I.34 | proposition | opposite sides of a parallelogram | vol. I, p. 323 |
| Elements I.47 | proposition | the square on the hypotenuse | vol. I, p. 349 |
| Elements VI.6 | proposition | one equal angle between proportional sides: the triangles are equiangular | vol. II, p. 204 |
| lemma same ray | lemma | equal distances from a point along parallels to one line, the same way: one point | rests on I.30 and I.3 |
| lemma same angle | lemma | equal angles with a ray on the same side: one direction | rests on I.23 |
| lemma segment addition | lemma | a point between two others: the whole is the sum of the parts | rests on Common Notion 4 |
| lemma one line | lemma | three points on one line: the lines through them are one | rests on Definition I.23 |
| trigonometry sine, cosine, tangent | definition | the right triangle | — |
| common notion 1 | common notion | things equal to the same thing are equal | vol. I, p. 155 |
| common notion 5 | common notion | the whole is greater than the part | vol. I, p. 155 |
| construction, algebra, expansion, cases | — | the other licences | — |

: The rules of derivation (`spec/rules.yaml`). The wording of each proposition was compared with the page of
Heath's translation in the Famulus collection; the lemmas are the author's. {#tbl-rules}

### What the figure supplies

Euclid's propositions take something from the figure that their enunciations do not say: that points are
distinct, that two points lie on the same side of a line, that two segments point the same way. In the rule
table these are **configuration conditions**. I.33 has two: the equal and parallel lines point the same way,
and three of the four points are not on one line. I.28 needs the two points on the same side of the
transversal. The conditions are evaluated in every sampled configuration. A state in which a condition fails
is a **degenerate state** of the step: the rule does not apply there.

For the argument of Fig. 1.2 this matters. At the apsides, κ̄ = 0° and κ̄ = 180°, the points O, E, S and C lie
on the apsidal line. There is no parallelogram, I.28 has no side to speak of, and I.33 has no figure. The
conclusion is true there as well: the sun of both forms is at the apogee, or at the perigee. But the argument
by the parallelogram does not reach it, and the printed figure, drawn at κ̄ ≈ 41°, does not show that it does
not (@fig-deg). An earlier version of the derivation (paper version 1.2) had the parallelogram steps alone,
and the checks reported the two states as degenerate. The derivation now distinguishes three cases. The first
has the condition that O, E and S are not on one line; the other two argue with lengths along the apsidal
line. With them no state is left uncovered (@lst-dcheck), and leaving one out is reported (@lst-fail).

![Left, the parallelogram OESC at κ̄ = 41°, with OE and CS in gold (step d6). Right, the same at κ̄ = 0°: the
four points lie on the apsidal line, and Elements I.33 has nothing to apply to.](figures/degenerate.png){#fig-deg width=92%}

### The proportional case

Under the hypothesis R′/r = R/e the two suns do not coincide, and the argument is another one. The triangles
OES and S′CO have an equal angle at E and at C, both the supplement of κ̄, between proportional sides,
e/R = r/R′. So they are equiangular (Elements VI.6: "if two triangles have one angle equal to one angle and
the sides about the equal angles proportional, the triangles will be equiangular"), and the angle at O equals
the angle at S′. That angle is the alternate angle of S′OE, since CS′ is parallel to OE (I.29). So S and S′
make the same angle with OE on the same side, and are seen from O in the same direction. Eleven steps and the conclusion: six are read off the constructions, one is a calculation, the others are
instances of rules or of Common Notion 1. The conclusion is
`equivalent(Ecc, Epi, direction)`. The same two states are degenerate.

### A derivation with an auxiliary construction

The page prints, without derivation, the equation of centre as a function of the mean anomaly:
tan c = e sin κ̄ / (R + e cos κ̄), and its sine form (p. 22). @lst-equation derives both in the eccentric model
alone. The derivation is supplied by the author of the astra text, and its header says so; it is not
attributed to the witness.

::: {#lst-equation}

```
derivation solar-equation-of-centre in solar-eccentric "The equation of centre
      is the angle at the sun, with tan c = e sin κ̄ / (R + e cos κ̄)"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p03 "the formula, p. 22"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05 "the maximum, p. 22"
author "Gerd Graßhoff"
construct F = foot(O, line(E, S)) "foot of the perpendicular from the earth on
      ES"
derive d1: equal(angle(S, E, A), angle(E, S, O) + angle(S, O, E)) from def A by
      Elements I.32 "OE produced to the apogee: the exterior angle at E of the
      triangle OES"
derive d2: equal(abs(norm180(c)), angle(O, S, E)) from d1, prop p1, prop p2 by
      algebra "the exterior angle is κ̄ and the angle at O is κ, so the angle at
      S is κ̄ − κ = c"
derive d3: perpendicular(line(O, F), line(E, S)) from def F by construction
derive d4: equal(distance(O, F), distance(O, E) * sin(angle(O, E, F))) from d3
      by trigonometry sine
derive d5: equal(distance(O, E), e) from def E by construction
derive d6: equal(sin(angle(O, E, F)), abs(sin(kappa_bar))) from def E, def S,
      def F by construction "the angle OEF is κ̄ or its supplement, according to
      the side of E on which F falls"
derive d7: equal(distance(O, F), e * abs(sin(kappa_bar))) from d4, d5, d6 by
      algebra
derive d8: equal(distance(S, F), R + e * cos(kappa_bar)) from def E, def S, def
      F by construction "F lies on SE produced beyond E for κ̄ within 90° of the
      apogee, between S and E otherwise: SF = SE ± EF"
derive d9: equal(tan(angle(O, S, F)), distance(O, F) / distance(S, F)) from d3
      by trigonometry tangent
derive d10: equal(angle(O, S, F), angle(O, S, E)) from def F, def E, def S by
      construction "F and E lie from S on one ray"
derive d11: equal(distance(O, F), distance(O, S) * sin(angle(O, S, F))) from d3
      by trigonometry sine
derive d12: equal(distance(O, S) * distance(O, S), distance(O, F) * distance(O,
      F) + distance(S, F) * distance(S, F)) from d3 by Elements I.47
conclude equal(tan(abs(norm180(c))), e * abs(sin(kappa_bar)) / (R + e *
      cos(kappa_bar))) from d2, d9, d10, d7, d8 by algebra "tan c = e sin κ̄ /
      (R + e cos κ̄), in absolute value"
conclude equal(sin(abs(norm180(c))), e * abs(sin(kappa_bar)) / sqrt(R * R + e *
      e + 2 * R * e * cos(kappa_bar))) from d2, d10, d11, d12, d7, d8 by algebra
      "sin c = e sin κ̄ / (R² + e² + 2Re cos κ̄)^½, in absolute value"
compute c_max = max(angle(O, S, E)) with solar-eccentric.ptolemy reported 2;23°
      source fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05 "Ptolemy's maximum equation"
compute c_max_battani = max(angle(O, S, E)) with solar-eccentric.battani
      reported 1;59,10° source fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p06 "al-Battānī's
      maximum equation"
```

The derivation `solar-equation-of-centre`. `construct` adds the foot F of the perpendicular from the earth on
ES to the context. Two `compute` operations compare the largest equation with the values the page reports.
:::

![The scene of the derivation: the perpendicular OF from the earth on the line ES. The equation of centre c is
the angle at the sun.](figures/solar-fig-equation.png){#fig-equation width=58%}

The auxiliary point F does not belong to the model. It exists in the derivation's context only
(@fig-equation). The steps use the exterior angle of the triangle OES (I.32), the right triangles OFE and OFS
(the definitions of sine, cosine and tangent, and I.47 for OS), and three steps read off the construction. One
of these, that SF = R + e cos κ̄, hides a distinction of cases — F falls beyond E or between S and E — and is the
weakest step: it is confirmed over the parameter space and not derived. The report shows this by its grade.
Four states are degenerate: at 0° and 180° the triangle OES vanishes, at 90° and 270° F falls on E.

`compute` evaluates a quantity under a parametrisation and compares it with a reported value at the precision
in which that value is written. The largest equation under Ptolemy's values is 2;23°, as printed. Under
al-Battānī's values it is 1;59,9°; the page reports 1;59,10°.

### Parameters determined from observations

The page reports how Ptolemy found the two parameters: from the vernal equinox to the summer solstice 94½
days "with mean motion 93;9°", from the summer solstice to the autumnal equinox 92½ days "with mean motion
91;11°", "from which he finds that where R = 60, e = 2;29,30 ≈ 2;30 … and λ~A~ = 65;30°" (p. 22). The operation
`determine` inverts the model numerically for the unknown parameters, given the time or the arc of mean motion
in which an observable goes from one value to another, and reports whether the solution it finds is the only
one (@lst-seasons).

::: {#lst-seasons}

```
$ famulus-astra derive solar-parameters-from-seasons
  = days.e: 2;28,58 at the only solution found; days turned into arcs of mean
        motion with T = 365;14,48 d; reported 2;29,30: discrepancy, difference
        −0;0,31,44
  = days.lamA: 65;26° at the only solution found; days turned into arcs of mean
        motion with T = 365;14,48 d; reported 65;30°: discrepancy, difference
        −0;3,47
  = arcs.e: 2;29,28 at the only solution found; the intervals taken as arcs of
        mean motion; reported 2;29,30: discrepancy, difference −0;0,1,51
  = arcs.lamA: 65;35° at the only solution found; the intervals taken as arcs of
        mean motion; reported 65;30°: discrepancy, difference +0;5,7
```

Eccentricity and apogee determined through the model, from the day counts and from the reported arcs.
:::

From the two reported arcs the model gives e = 2;29,28 and λ~A~ = 65;35°. From the day counts, turned into mean
motion with the year of 365;14,48 days and exact arithmetic, it gives e = 2;28,58 and λ~A~ = 65;26°. Ptolemy's
2;29,30 and 65;30° agree with the first pair to within what a computation with a table of chords loses, and not
with the second. The reported parameters follow from the rounded arcs. Swerdlow's paragraph says of this
derivation that it "is so sensitive to errors in the intervals of time … that the exact results are
fortuitous"; the two determinations show the sensitivity within Ptolemy's own numbers. Ptolemy's steps are not
on the page, and Almagest III 4 is not among the texts available to us in addressable form: a gap.

### What a derivation is and is not

A derivation that passes the four checks is reasoning whose every proposition is true in the model and whose
every step names a rule that licenses it. It is not a formal proof in an axiomatic system. Truth is tested on
samples. Rules are matched as schemas, and the schema states one case of a proposition. Configuration
conditions are evaluated numerically. What the checks give is what a careful reader of a geometrical argument
wants to know: which proposition licenses this step, are its hypotheses all there, where does it not apply,
and does the conclusion use something that was never said.

## What follows from the texts {#sec-derived}

### Sheets

Every text has a specification sheet that restates it as tables: for a model the parameter symbols, the
kinematic variable, the construction, the observables and the stated propositions with their check; for a
parametrisation the values with their sources (@lst-sheet); for a derivation the givens, the steps with
premises, rule, status, binding, configuration conditions and degenerate states, the rules with Heath's wording
and page, and the computed quantities. A sheet does not mix kinds: a model's sheet has no values, and names its
parametrisations, figures and derivations by address.

::: {#lst-sheet}

```yaml
address: fr:astra/solar-eccentric.ptolemy
kind: parametrisation
title: Ptolemy's values
attributed: Ptolemy
witness:
- address: fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05
  note: ''
author: Gerd Graßhoff
of: fr:astra/solar-eccentric
check:
  level: attested
  complete: true
values:
- symbol: R
  display: R
  meaning: radius of the eccentric
  as_written: '60'
  value: 60.0
  sexagesimal: '60'
  unit: length
  note: the norm of the radius
  source: fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05
  gap: null
  taken_from: null
- symbol: e
  display: e
  meaning: eccentricity, the distance OE
  as_written: 2;30
  value: 2.5
  sexagesimal: 2;30
  unit: length
  note: 2;29,30 rounded to 2;30
  source: fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0052/p05
  gap: null
  taken_from: null
gaps:
- 'kappa_0 = 0°: the pages give no epoch; t is counted from a passage through
      the apogee'
…
```

The YAML sheet of the parametrisation `fr:astra/solar-eccentric.ptolemy`, abridged to its head, its first two values and its gaps.
:::

### Figures and animation

A figure text says what it draws (@lst-figure). Fig. 1.2 and Fig. 1.4 are two figure texts; the second draws
the eccentric model alone, under the same values, at κ̄ = 108°, with the solstitial colure upright
(@fig-complete). Drawing values replace attested ones for legibility; they stand in the figure text, appear in
the corner of the figure and never touch a parametrisation.

::: {#lst-figure}

```
figure solar-fig-1-2 of solar-eccentric-epicyclic "The two forms of Ptolemy's
      solar model superposed, R' = R and r = e"
witness fr:kepler-sec/Swerdlow2026/0050/fig01
author "Gerd Graßhoff"
with solar-eccentric-epicyclic.equal-radii
drawn with Ecc.e = 16, Epi.r = 16
at kappa_bar = 41°
up lamA
fit [Ecc.ecc, Epi.def, Epi.epi]
speed 24 s
display Epi.S' "S'"
label angle kappa_bar at Ecc.E from Ecc.A to Ecc.S arrow
label angle kappa_bar at O from Epi.A' to Epi.C arrow
label length Ecc.R on Ecc.E Ecc.S
label length Ecc.e on O Ecc.E
label length Epi.R' on O Epi.C side right
label length Epi.r on Epi.C Epi.S' side right
label segment line(Ecc.A, Ecc.B)
label arrow Ecc.S on Ecc.ecc
label arrow Epi.C on Epi.def
label dashed line(O, Epi.S')
```

The figure text for Swerdlow's Fig. 1.2.
:::

![Swerdlow's Fig. 1.4 drawn from the figure text `solar-fig-1-4`: the eccentric model under Ptolemy's values at
κ̄ = 108°, with the longitudes λ~S~ and λ̄~S~ as directions from O, the angles λ~A~, κ̄ and κ, the equation of centre c
at S and at O, and the cardinal points of the zodiac. It is the model of @fig-two in another state and another
view.](figures/solar-fig-1-4.png){#fig-complete width=62%}

The same figure text gives the moving figure; nothing is added to it.

{{< famulus fr:astra/solar-fig-1-4 animation >}}

The animation is a page with the figure in its depicted state, a bundle — the context's operations, the
figure's parameter values, the scene — and a runtime of 468 lines of JavaScript without libraries. The runtime
computes configurations with the same operations as the Python evaluator and redraws the figure at every
frame. It has one control per thing to do: a slider for the kinematic variable; for a superposition a slider
that fades between the two members, which is Duke's demonstration of the equivalence [@duke-models]; for a
figure of a derivation two buttons that go through the steps, each shown with its proposition, rule and status
and with what it speaks of in gold.

Two evaluators of one operation set can drift apart. They are held together by shared cases: 64 written by
hand, at least one per operation including the cases without solution, and the values of every named object of
every context under sampled and attested parameter values in eight states, six given by the variable and two
by a time. On 10 November 2026 all 3,914 values agreed to 10⁻⁹. A headless browser photographs an animation at
three moments and requires the frames to differ.

### Reference objects {#sec-references}

Famulus gives every object it keeps an address beginning with `fr:` [@grasshoff2026aischolar]. Every astra
text and every part of it has one: `fr:astra/solar-eccentric` is the model, `fr:astra/solar-eccentric.ptolemy/e`
a value with its source, `fr:astra/solar-eccentric-epicyclic/Epi.C` the centre of the epicycle in the
superposition, `fr:astra/solar-equivalence-equal-radii/d6` a step (@lst-describe). Prose elsewhere can then say
exactly what it speaks of.

::: {#lst-describe}

```json
{
 "ref": "fr:astra/solar-equivalence-equal-radii/d6",
 "kind": "astra_step",
 "found": true,
 "proposition": "parallel(line(O, Ecc.E), line(Epi.C, Ecc.S))",
 "from": [
  "sides",
  "d5"
 ],
 "by": "Elements I.33",
 "status": "justified",
 "binding": {
  "A": "O",
  "C": "Ecc.E",
  "B": "Epi.C",
  "D": "Ecc.S"
 },
 "hypotheses_met_by": {
  "parallel(line(A, B), line(C, D))": "sides",
  "equal(distance(A, B), distance(C, D))": "d5"
 },
 "configuration_conditions": [
  "same_direction(O, Epi.C, Ecc.E, Ecc.S)",
  "noncollinear(O, Epi.C, Ecc.E)"
 ],
 "degenerate_at": null,
 "in": "fr:astra/solar-equivalence-equal-radii"
}
```

The reference service answering for step d6.
:::

### Comparing {#sec-compare}

Two models are compared for identity, two parametrisations value by value (@lst-diff). The comparison states
and does not interpret. That two different models agree in what they show is not something it can say.

::: {#lst-diff}

```
$ famulus-astra diff solar-eccentric solar-epicyclic
solar-eccentric ↔ solar-epicyclic: different geometries
  parameters
    − R
    − e
    + R'
    + r
  construction
    − E (point) = O + polar(e, lamA)
    − ecc (circle) = circle(E, R)
    − A (point) = E + polar(R, lamA)
    − B (point) = E - polar(R, lamA)
    − S (point) = E + polar(R, lamA + kappa_bar)
    + def (circle) = circle(O, R')
    + A' (point) = O + polar(R', lamA)
    + B' (point) = O - polar(R', lamA)
    + C (point) = O + polar(R', lamA + kappa_bar)
    + epi (circle) = circle(C, r)
    + S' (point) = C + polar(r, lamA)
  observables
    − lam_bar (angle) = direction(E, S)
    − kappa (angle) = lam - lamA
    − c (angle) = lam_bar - lam
    ~ lam: direction(O, S) → direction(O, S')
  shared construction: O

$ famulus-astra diff solar-eccentric.ptolemy solar-eccentric.battani
solar-eccentric.ptolemy ↔ solar-eccentric.battani
  values of Ptolemy ↔ al-Battānī
    R: 60 ↔ 60  (equal)
    e: 2;30 ↔ 2;4,45  (−0;25,15)
    lamA: 65;30° ↔ 82;17°  (+16;47)
    T: 365;14,48 ↔ —
    kappa_0: 0° ↔ —
```

The two forms are different geometries that share only the observer. The two parametrisations differ in the
eccentricity and in the apogee, the latter by 16;47°.
:::

## Case study: Ptolemy's solar model in Swerdlow's exposition {#sec-case}

@tbl-texts lists the eighteen texts. All but one pass every level of their check. The exception is
al-Battānī's parametrisation, which is incomplete because the page is.

| text | kind | what it is |
|---|---|---|
| `solar-eccentric`, `solar-epicyclic` | model | the two forms; 54 and 40 operations |
| `solar-eccentric.ptolemy`, `.battani` | parametrisation | Ptolemy's values; al-Battānī's (partial) |
| `solar-eccentric-epicyclic` | superposition | the scene of Figs. 1.2 and 1.3; 84 operations |
| `…epicyclic.equal-radii`, `.proportional` | parametrisation | Ptolemy's values with the radii of Fig. 1.2 and of Fig. 1.3 |
| `solar-equivalence-equal-radii` | derivation | 2 givens, 26 steps in 3 cases; equivalence in position |
| `solar-equivalence-proportional` | derivation | 1 given, 11 steps; equivalence in direction |
| `solar-equation-of-centre` | derivation | 1 auxiliary construction, 12 steps, 2 conclusions, 2 computations |
| `solar-parameters-from-seasons` | derivation | 2 determinations |
| `solar-fig-1-2`, `-1-3`, `-1-4` and four more | figure | the three printed figures; the members alone; the scenes of two derivations |

: The texts of the case study (`examples/solar/`). {#tbl-texts}

Writing the texts was a reading of the pages. It found the following.

**The parallelogram argument does not cover the apsides.** Steps by I.28 and I.33 have configuration
conditions that fail at κ̄ = 0° and 180°. The page's sentence "complete parallelogram OESC" presupposes a
position in which there is one. The complete argument has three cases, of which page and figure give one.

**The reported parameters follow from the rounded arcs.** Inverting the model for the reported arcs gives
Ptolemy's eccentricity to two units of the second sexagesimal place and his apogee to 0;5°; inverting it for
the day counts does not.

**A misprinted sign rule and a misprinted difference.** On page 22 the rule for the sign of c reads "for κ̄ >
180°" in both clauses; the model gives c > 0° for κ̄ < 180°. The same page gives the advance of al-Battānī's
apogee over Ptolemy's as 16;37°; the two longitudes on the page differ by 16;47°, as the comparison of the two
parametrisations shows (@lst-diff). Both are treated in a companion paper that reads these pages assertion by
assertion [@grasshoff2026deepreading].

**Errors of our own.** The first draft gave the year the locus of page 19, which prints the mean motion of the
Alfonsine Tables; the Ptolemaic year stands on page 17. A source being named is not a source being right, and
the address makes the check a quick one. The second version (1.1) of this paper made the four conflations
listed in @sec-intro. And a figure in the companion paper, al-Battānī's largest equation recomputed as
1;59,8°, was truncated where it should have been rounded: it is 1;59,9°.

## Representation, model, description {#sec-repr}

The **model** is not a text. It is the geometry: the function from parameter values and states to
configurations, with what holds of it. Ptolemy's eccentric solar model is the same model whether Ptolemy,
Regiomontanus, Swerdlow or an astra text states it. That is why a model text names three persons or texts and
not one.

A **representation** is anything that determines the model: Ptolemy's prose and figure, Swerdlow's prose and
figures, the astra model text, the operation list, the sheet. Two representations represent the same model
when they agree in every configuration. A **figure** represents less and more than the model. Less, because it
shows one state under one set of values. More, because it chooses a view, and often values that are not the
attested ones. Figs. 1.2 and 1.4 draw one model in two states, two orientations and with an exaggerated
eccentricity.

A **diagram of two models** is a superposition. Fig. 1.2 is drawn so that a parallelogram can be seen, and
the parallelogram is the place of an argument. Three things are to be told apart: the superposition, which the
diagram shows; the hypotheses under which something holds across the two models, under which the diagram is
drawn and which it cannot show; and the derivation, whose scene the diagram is. The equivalence of the two
models is the conclusion of the third. It is a relation between models, not between representations of one
model.

The **describing text** refers to the model through names — "complete parallelogram OESC", "r is always
parallel to the apsidal line AB" — and asserts things of different kinds. The astra texts make the names
resolvable and each assertion checkable as what it is (@tbl-assert).

| the prose | is checked as |
|---|---|
| states a property of the construction | a proposition of the model |
| says that something follows | a step of a derivation, with its rule |
| gives a number that follows from other numbers | arithmetic, or a quantity computed under a parametrisation |
| gives parameters found from observations | a determination through the model |

: Kinds of assertion in a describing text, and how each is checked. {#tbl-assert}

## Related work {#sec-related}

Languages for geometrical constructions are older than dynamic geometry on screens. **GCLC**, the Geometry
Constructions Language of Janičić [@janicic2010gclc], treats a construction as a procedure and has a built-in
prover that takes a conjecture about the construction and returns a proof. astra/1 takes from GCLC the view of
a construction as a procedure whose objects depend only on earlier ones. Its derivations do something
different from GCLC's prover: they take the steps that a historical text gives or presupposes, with the
propositions that text would cite, and ask of each step whether it is true and whether that proposition
licenses it. The aim is to analyse a given piece of reasoning, not to find a proof. **Eukleides**
[@obrecht2002eukleides] makes the text resemble geometric notation, works with typed values and avoids
Cartesian coordinates where it can; astra/1 is typed with units and has no coordinates in its texts.
**Cinderella** [@richtergebert2012cinderella], **GeoGebra** [@hohenwarter2002geogebra] and **JSXGraph**
[@gerhauser2010jsxgraph] made constructions interactive: a free element or a slider drives a graph of
dependent objects. In such a construction, values and geometry are stored together; astra/1 separates them.

Animations of historical models are a separate tradition. Dennis Duke's animations of the Almagest models
[@duke-models] compute every position in closed form from one time variable and demonstrate the equivalence of
eccentric and epicyclic forms by a slider that turns one into the other. Brandon Rhodes' scale drawings of
Ptolemy's cosmos [@rhodes-ptolemy] fit their parameters to a modern ephemeris and state their scale
conventions. astra/1 shares the fade between two models with Duke and the stated conventions with Rhodes, and
adds what neither aims at: the geometry apart from the values, a source for each number, the equivalence as a
derivation with hypotheses, and an address for every part. The Famulus astronomy expert
[@grasshoff2026famulusastronomy] computes positions from the Almagest models; astra/1 describes the
constructions those computations presuppose.

@tbl-compare summarises the differences. It records design aims, not quality: the geometry packages are
general-purpose tools, astra/1 is a language for one kind of object.

| | GCLC | Eukleides | Cinderella, GeoGebra, JSXGraph | Duke | astra/1 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| a construction is a text | yes | yes | stored files | code | yes |
| coordinates | yes | avoided | yes | yes | only the observer |
| units typed | no | types, no units | no | no | yes |
| geometry apart from values | no | no | no | no | model and parametrisation |
| two models in one scene | no | no | by hand | slider | superposition |
| reasoning | prover finds a proof | no | no | no | a given derivation is checked step by step |
| sources | no | no | no | no | required or gap |
| citable parts | no | no | no | no | `fr:astra/…` |

: Design aims of astra/1 and the work it learned from. {#tbl-compare}

## Limitations {#sec-limits}

Cases have to be found by the author of a derivation: the checks report the states a step does not cover
and whether the cases cover every configuration, but they do not propose the distinction. The cases hidden in
a step that is read off the construction are not shown. Hypotheses have to fix a parameter; inequalities
between parameters and hypotheses of incidence are not supported. The grades *read off the construction* and
*calculation* are tested numerically and not replayed symbolically; an expansion is tested at three sizes of
the small ratio and not derived term by term. A rule schema states one case of a proposition, and the rule table
holds the rules that the derivations written so far needed. Truth is tested on samples throughout.

The language is planar; latitude needs a third dimension. Motions taken from tables are not implemented. Only
the solar model is written. The language covers the equant (`seen_from`), the Tusi couple (`rotate`) and
Kepler's ellipse (`true_anomaly`, `ellipse`), but no such model is written, because the rule that every value
has a source that can be addressed is kept: the later chapters of Swerdlow, which announce "more interesting
transformations of planetary models based upon the same principles that lead from the Ptolemaic to the
Copernican and Tychonic systems" (p. 20), are not yet read for loci. They are superpositions and derivations
waiting to be written.

The level *attested* checks that a source is named, not that it says what the parametrisation says. The
propositions of Euclid are cited by the page of Heath's translation, not by paragraph, because those volumes
have no paragraph addressing yet. The Almagest itself is not among the addressable texts, so the attribution
to Ptolemy and the loci in the Almagest are as Swerdlow's page gives them. The code is not yet public.

## Conclusion

A historical astronomical model can be written so that four things stay apart: the geometry, as a model
without values; the numbers, as parametrisations with owners and sources; the position, as a state; and the
reasoning, as a derivation. A diagram that shows two models is then a superposition, and what it is drawn to
make visible, an equivalence, is the conclusion of a derivation under hypotheses the diagram cannot show. A
derivation whose steps name premises and rules can be checked step by step: for truth over the parameter
space, for whether the step is an instance of its rule, for the states in which the rule does not apply, and
for premises used without being said. Applied to Ptolemy's solar model as Swerdlow uses it, this showed that
the parallelogram argument needs two further cases at the apsides and that the reported parameters follow from rounded arcs.
Neither is visible in the diagram.

## Availability {.unnumbered}

The language specification (`spec/astra-1.md`), the operation set, the rules of derivation, the test cases,
the eighteen texts, the runtime, the deep-reading tool and the tests are in the Famulus workspace
`famulus-astronomica` (41 tests; 6,467 lines of Python and JavaScript, of which 952 for derivations and rules,
468 for the runtime and 354 for the deep-reading tool). The addresses `fr:astra/…` resolve on the Famulus
reference service of the author's node; the names used in version 1.1 of this paper resolve to the objects
they have become. Swerdlow's book is cited by its scan pages in the Famulus collection `kepler-sec` (scan page n
is printed page n − 30 in chapter 1); Heath's Euclid by the scan pages of the collection `assyrischeSammlung`.
